/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cism.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cism", [["cm1",0,1,"A newly hired information security manager is asked to build a security program. What should the manager do FIRST?",["Select a control framework such as ISO/IEC 27001 and begin a full gap assessment against it","Purchase tools that address the most common attack techniques","Write a comprehensive set of security policies for approval","Learn the organization's business objectives, culture and risk appetite"],3,"Security governance exists to support business goals, so the manager must understand what the business is trying to achieve before choosing controls or tools. Jumping to a control framework or technology first produces a program that may not fit the organization.","Job practice 1A",["A gap assessment needs a defined desired state, and that comes from business objectives; picking a framework before understanding the business puts the method ahead of the goal.","Buying technology before understanding business needs and risk leads to spending that may not match what the organization actually needs to protect.","Policies express management intent; writing them before learning the business objectives and culture produces documents that may be rejected or ignored.",null],2],["cm2",0,1,"Who is ultimately accountable for information security in an organization?",["The chief information security officer","The IT operations director","The internal audit department","The board and executive management"],3,"Accountability for protecting information assets rests with the board and senior executives. The CISO leads and advises, and IT implements, but neither can hold the organization's ultimate accountability.","Job practice 1A",["The CISO is responsible for running the program and advising leadership, but responsibility can be delegated while ultimate accountability stays with the board and executives.","IT operations implements many technical controls, but implementing controls is custodial work, not ultimate accountability for the organization's information risk.","Internal audit provides independent assurance; if it were accountable for security it would be auditing its own work and lose independence.",null],1],["cm3",0,1,"Which outcome BEST shows that information security governance is effective?",["Security activities are aligned with and support business objectives","The number of approved security policies has increased","All servers have the latest security patches installed","The security budget grew faster than the overall IT budget for three years"],0,"Strategic alignment is the primary outcome of good security governance. The other choices can happen in a poorly governed program: many policies or tools do not prove the program serves the business.","Job practice 1A",[null,"A larger policy library measures activity, not effectiveness; policies can multiply without improving alignment or reducing risk.","Patch compliance is an operational metric; it says nothing about whether the security program as a whole supports business goals.","Budget growth shows investment, not value; money can be spent on activities that do not support the business."],1],["cm4",0,1,"An organization is expanding into a country with a strict data localization law. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Move all customer data to a data center inside that country","Ask the vendor of the current cloud service to certify compliance","Identify the law's requirements and how they affect operations","Update the acceptable use policy to reference the new law"],2,"Legal and regulatory requirements must be understood before choosing a response. Once the requirements and the gap are known, management can decide on controls, contracts or changes to the plan.","Job practice 1A",["Relocating data is a possible response, but deciding on it before understanding what the law actually requires may be costly and unnecessary.","A vendor attestation cannot confirm compliance until the organization knows which requirements apply to its own data flows.",null,"Changing a user-facing policy does not address where data is stored or processed, which is what a localization law regulates."],2],["cm5",0,1,"Which factor is MOST likely to cause a new security policy to be ignored by employees?",["It conflicts with the organization's culture and has no visible leadership support","It was reviewed by the legal department before it was published","It is written at a high level without naming specific products","It is supported by separate standards and procedures that employees must read first"],0,"Culture and leadership sponsorship drive adoption. A policy that clashes with how people work, and that leaders do not model or enforce, tends to be bypassed regardless of its technical quality.","Job practice 1A",[null,"Legal review improves a policy's defensibility; it does not make employees ignore it.","Policies are meant to be high-level statements of intent; specific technologies belong in standards, so this is normal and not a cause of noncompliance.","Supporting documents help people follow a policy; separating them is good practice, not a reason for it to be ignored."],2],["cm6",0,1,"A company's security steering committee is being formed. Which membership is MOST appropriate?",["The security team plus the network, server and database administrators from IT","Senior representatives from the major business units, IT, legal and risk","External auditors who review the security program each year","The CISO alone, reporting directly to the board each quarter"],1,"A steering committee brings business and support leaders together so that security decisions reflect business priorities. Limiting it to technical staff or to one function defeats its coordinating purpose.","Job practice 1A",["A committee of technical staff cannot make business-level trade-offs or commit business unit resources.",null,"External auditors must stay independent; sitting on the committee that directs the program would compromise their role.","A single person is not a committee; the value of a steering committee is cross-functional input and buy-in."],2],["cm7",0,1,"Who should decide the classification level of a customer database?",["The database administrator who maintains it","The information security manager","The business owner of the data","The chief information officer"],2,"Data owners are senior business managers who are accountable for the data, so they decide its classification and who may access it. Custodians and security staff advise and implement.","Job practice 1A",["The database administrator is a custodian who implements controls on the owner's behalf; custodians do not set classification.","The security manager defines the classification scheme and advises, but the business owner assigns the level for their data.",null,"The CIO manages IT delivery; classifying business data is a business ownership decision, not an IT management one."],1],["cm8",0,1,"When developing an information security strategy, what is the PRIMARY purpose of defining the desired state?",["To document the controls that are already operating today","To prove to auditors that every control is fully effective","To set a target for measuring the current state and gaps","To list every technical product that the program will purchase"],2,"A strategy moves the organization from its current state to a desired state. Defining the desired state gives the target that gap analysis compares against, and the gaps become the roadmap.","Job practice 1B",["That describes the current state, not the desired state; the strategy needs both to find the gap.","A desired state is a planning target, not evidence of current control effectiveness.",null,"Products are one possible means of closing gaps; the desired state is expressed as outcomes and capabilities, not a shopping list."],2],["cm9",0,1,"An information security manager needs senior management approval for a major new security initiative. What should the business case emphasize MOST?",["The technical features that make the proposed solution better than competing products","Recent breaches at competitors that used a similar product","How the initiative reduces risk to business objectives relative to its cost","The number of vulnerabilities currently open in the environment"],2,"Executives approve investments that support business goals at an acceptable cost. A business case framed in terms of business risk and value is far more persuasive than technical features or industry fear.","Job practice 1B",["Technical superiority matters to engineers, but executives decide based on business value and risk reduction.","Competitor breaches can add context, but fear-based arguments without analysis of the organization's own risk are weak justification.",null,"A raw vulnerability count does not show business impact or explain why this initiative is the right use of money."],2],["cm10",0,1,"Which governance framework is MOST closely associated with governance and management of enterprise IT, including aligning IT goals with enterprise goals?",["COBIT","ITIL","ISO/IEC 27002","OWASP ASVS"],0,"COBIT, published by ISACA, is a framework for the governance and management of enterprise information and technology, with a goals cascade that links enterprise goals to IT-related goals.","Job practice 1B",[null,"ITIL is a framework for IT service management practices, not primarily for enterprise governance of IT.","ISO/IEC 27002 is a catalog of information security controls and implementation guidance, not an enterprise IT governance framework.","The OWASP Application Security Verification Standard defines application security requirements, not enterprise governance."],1],["cm11",0,1,"An organization wants an internationally recognized certification that its information security management system (ISMS) meets a defined standard. Which standard should it use?",["ISO/IEC 27002","NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","COBIT 2019","ISO/IEC 27001"],3,"ISO/IEC 27001 specifies requirements for an ISMS, and organizations can be certified against it by an accredited body. ISO/IEC 27002 is guidance on controls and is not certifiable on its own.","Job practice 1B",["27002 provides control guidance that supports 27001, but organizations are not certified against 27002.","The NIST CSF is a voluntary framework for managing cybersecurity risk; there is no official certification against it.","COBIT is a governance framework used for assessment and improvement, but it does not certify an ISMS.",null],1],["cm12",0,1,"Which function was added in NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 to emphasize organizational context, strategy and oversight?",["Govern","Identify","Assure","Prepare"],0,"CSF 2.0 added the Govern function alongside Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. Govern covers organizational context, risk management strategy, roles, policy, oversight and supply chain risk management.","Job practice 1B",[null,"Identify existed in CSF 1.1; it covers asset management and risk assessment, while governance outcomes now sit in Govern.","Assure is not a CSF function; the six functions are Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover.","Prepare is a step in the NIST Risk Management Framework (SP 800-37), not a CSF 2.0 function."],1],["cm13",0,1,"Senior management has stated a low risk appetite for customer data exposure. How should this MOST directly influence the security strategy?",["All other security initiatives should be paused until the risk is eliminated","The organization should stop collecting any customer data at all","The security team should accept any risk it judges to be minor","Prioritize controls for customer data to keep residual risk within it"],3,"Risk appetite tells the program how much risk leadership is willing to accept. A low appetite for customer data exposure means strategy and funding should favor controls that keep residual risk in that area low.","Job practice 1B",["Risk is rarely eliminated; appetite guides priorities, it does not justify halting everything else.","Avoidance is one possible response to a specific risk, but a low appetite does not mean the business must abandon its core activities.","Risk acceptance belongs to management within the approved appetite and tolerance, not to the security team on its own judgment.",null],3],["cm14",0,1,"What is the MOST important factor when deciding how much to spend on information security?",["The value of the assets protected and the risk to business objectives","The average security spending of peer organizations in the same industry","The previous year's budget plus inflation","The total cost of the tools the security team prefers"],0,"Security spending should be proportionate to the value at risk and the organization's risk appetite. Spending based on benchmarks or last year's budget may be too much or too little for the actual risk.","Job practice 1B",[null,"Peer benchmarks give context but ignore the organization's own assets, threats and appetite.","Incremental budgeting does not reflect changes in the organization's risk or business direction.","Tool preferences are not a measure of business need; spending should follow risk, not wish lists."],2],["cm15",0,1,"A security manager learns that a regional office regularly ignores a group-wide policy because a local regulation requires something different. What is the BEST course of action?",["Require the office to follow the group policy regardless of local law","Allow the office to continue informally since the law requires it","Document the conflict and approve a risk-assessed exception","Rewrite the group policy to match the regional regulation everywhere"],2,"When a local legal requirement conflicts with group policy, the answer is a formal, documented variance: legal requirements are met and the risk of the difference is understood and approved.","Job practice 1A",["Forcing noncompliance with local law creates legal risk that is likely worse than the policy difference.","Informal deviations hide risk and weaken governance; exceptions must be documented and approved.",null,"Changing the policy for every location to fit one jurisdiction may impose unnecessary requirements on all the others."],3],["cm16",0,1,"Which is the BEST indicator that an information security strategy is successful?",["Business goals are being achieved with risk kept within the approved appetite","Every employee has completed annual security awareness training","The security team has deployed all tools listed in the roadmap","No security incidents of any kind were reported during the last twelve months"],0,"A strategy succeeds when the business meets its objectives while risk stays within appetite. Measures of activity, such as training completions or tools deployed, are inputs rather than outcomes.","Job practice 1B",[null,"Training completion is an activity measure; it does not show that the strategy is achieving its business goals.","Deploying tools is progress on tasks, not proof that risk is reduced or that business goals are supported.","Zero reported incidents may reflect weak detection rather than success, and some incidents are expected within appetite."],2],["cm17",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY reason to perform a risk assessment before selecting security controls?",["To satisfy the requirements of the external auditors","To find every vulnerability in every system the organization operates","To calculate the exact financial loss of every threat","To choose controls in proportion to the risks they address"],3,"Risk assessment identifies and prioritizes risks so that controls can be selected and funded in proportion to the risk. Without it, spending may go to low-risk areas while serious risks remain.","Job practice 2A",["Audit may expect a risk assessment, but the primary purpose is informed control selection, not satisfying auditors.","A risk assessment prioritizes risk; it is not an exhaustive vulnerability hunt, which vulnerability scanning supports.","Exact figures are rarely possible; risk assessment estimates likelihood and impact well enough to prioritize.",null],2],["cm18",0,2,"An asset is valued at $500,000. A flood would destroy 40% of it and is expected once every 20 years. What is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?",["$10,000","$200,000","$25,000","$4,000,000"],0,"Single loss expectancy (SLE) = asset value x exposure factor = 500,000 x 0.40 = 200,000. The annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) is 1/20 = 0.05. ALE = SLE x ARO = 200,000 x 0.05 = 10,000.","Job practice 2A",[null,"$200,000 is the SLE; it must be multiplied by the ARO of 0.05 to become an annual figure.","$25,000 would be the asset value times the ARO, ignoring the 40% exposure factor.","$4,000,000 comes from multiplying the SLE by 20 instead of dividing by 20."],3],["cm19",0,2,"When is qualitative risk analysis MORE appropriate than quantitative analysis?",["When reliable data on loss amounts and frequencies is not available","When the board requires a cost-benefit analysis in dollars","When an insurance premium must be justified against expected annual loss","When the organization has years of detailed loss history"],0,"Qualitative analysis ranks risk using scales such as high, medium and low. It is useful when solid numbers are missing or when a quick prioritization is needed. Quantitative analysis needs credible financial data.","Job practice 2A",[null,"A dollar-based cost-benefit analysis calls for quantitative analysis, not qualitative.","Justifying a premium against expected loss is a quantitative exercise using figures like ALE.","Good historical loss data is exactly what makes quantitative analysis practical and credible."],2],["cm20",0,2,"A risk assessment identifies that a legacy application cannot be patched. What should the information security manager do NEXT?",["Immediately shut down the legacy application","Accept the risk on behalf of the business owner","Analyze the risk and evaluate compensating controls","Report the unpatchable application to the external auditors for their opinion"],2,"When a vulnerability cannot be fixed directly, the next step is to analyze the resulting risk and consider compensating controls such as isolation or monitoring, then present the options to the business owner.","Job practice 2A",["Shutting down a business application without analysis and owner input may cause more harm than the vulnerability.","The security manager does not accept risk for the owner; acceptance is a documented business decision.",null,"Reporting to external auditors is not a risk treatment step and skips analysis and internal decision-making."],3],["cm21",0,2,"Which element is MOST essential in a well-written risk scenario?",["The name of the tool that will be used to detect the event","A threat exploiting a weakness in an asset, with business impact","The exact date the event is expected to occur","A list of every control that currently exists in the environment"],1,"A risk scenario describes a plausible event: who or what (the threat), how (the vulnerability or weakness), what is affected (the asset) and the business impact. This lets management understand and compare risks.","Job practice 2A",["Detection tools are a treatment detail, not part of describing the risk itself.",null,"Risk deals with likelihood over a period, not a predicted date.","Relevant existing controls affect the scenario's likelihood, but a full control inventory is not the scenario's essential element."],2],["cm22",0,2,"A threat intelligence report describes a new ransomware group targeting the organization's industry. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Purchase a new endpoint detection product immediately","Assess exposure to the group's known techniques","Notify all customers that an attack may be coming","Disconnect the network from the internet as a precaution"],1,"New threat information should feed the risk process: first determine relevance and exposure, then decide whether controls or priorities should change. Reacting without assessment wastes resources or misses the real gap.","Job practice 2A",["Buying a product before knowing the exposure may not address the techniques the group actually uses.",null,"Notifying customers without evidence of an attack causes confusion and is not a risk management step.","Disconnecting the business without assessed need would cause a self-inflicted outage."],3],["cm23",0,2,"What does a control deficiency analysis MOST directly identify?",["Which employees have violated a security policy during the year","The total cost of the security program by category","Gaps between required controls and those working effectively","The attackers most likely to target the organization"],2,"Control deficiency analysis compares required control objectives to what exists and works, revealing gaps in design or operation that increase risk.","Job practice 2A",["Policy violations are compliance incidents, not a comparison of required versus operating controls.","Cost breakdowns support budgeting, not control gap analysis.",null,"Threat actor profiling is threat analysis; deficiency analysis looks at the organization's own controls."],2],["cm24",0,2,"A business owner decides not to implement a recommended control because the cost is higher than the expected loss. Which risk response is this?",["Risk avoidance","Risk transfer","Risk acceptance","Risk mitigation"],2,"Choosing to live with a risk after informed analysis, often because treatment costs more than the expected loss, is risk acceptance. It should be documented and approved by someone with the authority to accept the risk.","Job practice 2B",["Avoidance means stopping the activity that creates the risk; here the activity continues.","Transfer shifts financial impact to another party, such as an insurer; no one else is taking on the risk here.",null,"Mitigation means applying controls to reduce the risk, which the owner decided not to do."],1],["cm25",0,2,"An organization buys cyber insurance to cover the cost of a data breach. Which statement about this response is MOST accurate?",["It removes the organization's legal responsibility for the data","It transfers some financial impact but not accountability","It eliminates the need for technical controls on the covered systems","It reduces the likelihood that a breach will occur"],1,"Insurance transfers some financial loss to the insurer. The organization still owns the data, is still accountable to regulators and customers, and still suffers reputational harm.","Job practice 2B",["Legal obligations to protect data remain with the organization; insurance only helps pay for some losses.",null,"Insurers usually require baseline controls, and insurance does not prevent breaches from happening.","Insurance affects the financial impact after an event, not the probability of the event."],3],["cm26",0,2,"Who should own a risk that affects the online ordering process?",["The information security manager who identified the risk","The system administrator who maintains the web servers","The business manager accountable for the online ordering process","The chief risk officer, who oversees the enterprise risk framework"],2,"Risk ownership belongs with the manager who is accountable for the business process and has authority to fund and approve its treatment. Security facilitates and advises.","Job practice 2B",["Identifying a risk does not make security its owner; security lacks authority over the business process and its budget.","Administrators often own specific controls, but they cannot make business decisions about the process's risk.",null,"The CRO oversees the risk framework and reporting; individual risks are owned by the accountable business managers."],2],["cm27",0,2,"What is the difference between risk ownership and control ownership?",["They are the same role with two different names","The risk owner answers for the risk; a control owner runs a control","A control owner must always be more senior than a risk owner","Risk owners always sit in IT and control owners always sit in the business"],1,"The risk owner is accountable for deciding how the risk is treated and whether residual risk is acceptable. A control owner is responsible for designing, operating or maintaining a particular control that helps treat that risk.","Job practice 2B",["They are different roles: one decides on the risk, the other operates a control; one person may hold both, but the roles differ.",null,"Control owners are often operational staff; risk owners are usually managers with budget authority.","It is usually the reverse: business managers own risks, while IT or other teams often own controls."],3],["cm28",0,2,"Which is the BEST example of a key risk indicator (KRI)?",["The number of security staff who hold certifications","The total number of firewall rules configured","The date of the last policy review meeting","Percentage of critical systems with overdue patches"],3,"A KRI is a forward-looking measure that signals rising risk exposure. Overdue patching on critical systems directly indicates increasing likelihood of compromise.","Job practice 2B",["Staff certifications describe capability, not a change in risk exposure.","A rule count shows configuration size, not whether risk is rising or falling.","A meeting date is a governance activity record, not an indicator of exposure.",null],2],["cm29",0,2,"Residual risk for a system remains above the risk appetite after all planned controls are implemented. What should the information security manager do?",["Accept the residual risk yourself because every planned control is in place","Escalate it to senior management to treat further or formally accept","Continue adding controls until the risk reaches zero","Lower the risk rating so it falls within the appetite"],1,"Residual risk above appetite needs a management decision: additional treatment, a change to the activity, or a documented acceptance by someone with the authority to do so.","Job practice 2B",["Completing the plan does not make the leftover risk acceptable; it is above appetite and needs a management decision.",null,"Zero risk is not achievable, and unlimited controls would cost more than the risk justifies.","Changing the rating without changing the risk misrepresents exposure to management."],3],["cm30",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of a risk register?",["To list every IT asset along with its serial number","To track each security incident from detection to closure","To document the configuration standards for servers","To record risks, owners, ratings and treatment status"],3,"A risk register is the central record of risks, their analysis, owners, responses and status. It supports monitoring and reporting over time.","Job practice 2B",["That is an asset inventory; a risk register records risks, not hardware details.","Incident tracking belongs in an incident management system; a register records potential future events.","Configuration standards are documented separately as standards or baselines.",null],1],["cm31",0,2,"How often should an organization's risk assessment be updated?",["Only when an external auditor asks for it during the annual audit cycle","Once, when the security program is first created","Periodically and after significant business or threat changes","Only after a major security incident has occurred"],2,"Risk changes as the business, technology and threats change. Assessments should be refreshed on a regular schedule and also when significant events happen, such as a merger, a new system or a new threat.","Job practice 2B",["Waiting for auditors leaves risk information stale between audits.","A one-time assessment quickly becomes outdated as the organization changes.",null,"Updating only after incidents is reactive and misses changes that could have prevented them."],2],["cm32",0,2,"When reporting risk to the board, what should the information security manager emphasize?",["Detailed vulnerability scan results for each server, sorted by severity","Top risks in business terms, trends and position against appetite","The technical architecture of the security tools","A complete list of every risk in the register"],1,"Boards need concise, business-focused information: the most significant risks, their potential impact on objectives, trends and whether exposure is within appetite, so they can make decisions.","Job practice 2B",["Server-level technical detail is too granular for board decision-making.",null,"Tool architecture does not help the board understand business risk or make decisions.","A full register overwhelms the board; it needs the significant risks and trends."],2],["cm33",0,2,"A vendor penetration test reports a critical finding on an internet-facing server. How should the finding be rated for business risk?",["Rate it critical because the tester labeled it critical in the report","Rate it low because the server passed its last audit","Rate it using the number of pages in the test report","Weigh business value, exploitability and existing controls"],3,"Technical severity alone does not equal business risk. The asset's importance, how exploitable the flaw is in context, and compensating controls determine the risk rating that drives priority.","Job practice 2A",["A tester's technical rating does not include business context; risk must reflect impact to the organization.","Passing a past audit does not mean a newly found flaw is low risk.","Report length has no relationship to the risk of a specific finding.",null],3],["cm34",0,2,"Which risk analysis approach combines numeric scales with descriptive categories, such as scoring likelihood and impact from 1 to 5?",["Quantitative analysis","Semi-quantitative analysis","Qualitative analysis","Monte Carlo simulation"],1,"Semi-quantitative analysis assigns numbers to qualitative categories so risks can be ranked and combined, without claiming the numbers represent actual money or probabilities.","Job practice 2A",["Quantitative analysis uses measured values such as dollars and frequencies, not ordinal 1-5 scores.",null,"Pure qualitative analysis uses descriptive ratings only, such as high, medium and low, without numeric scoring.","Monte Carlo is a quantitative technique that models ranges of values through repeated random sampling."],3],["cm35",0,3,"What should be completed FIRST when building an information asset classification program?",["Encryption of all data classified as confidential","An inventory of information assets with identified owners","Purchase and deployment of a data loss prevention (DLP) product","Training users on how to label documents"],1,"You cannot classify what you have not identified. An inventory with named owners gives the scope, and owners then classify their assets using the scheme.","Job practice 3A",["Encryption is a handling control applied after classification; without classification you do not know what needs it.",null,"A DLP product relies on classification rules and labels, so buying it first puts the tool ahead of the program.","Labeling training comes after the scheme, inventory and ownership are established."],1],["cm36",0,3,"Which factor should MOST influence the classification level assigned to an information asset?",["The cost of the storage system on which the asset is currently kept","The harm to the business if it were disclosed, altered or lost","The number of employees who currently use it","The department in which the asset was created"],1,"Classification reflects the asset's value and sensitivity, measured by the harm that would occur if it were disclosed, altered or unavailable. That drives how strongly it must be protected.","Job practice 3A",["Storage cost is unrelated to how sensitive the information is.",null,"Many people may use low-sensitivity data, and few may use highly sensitive data; user count is not the measure.","The creating department does not determine sensitivity; business impact does."],2],["cm37",0,3,"A policy states: 'All information must be protected according to its classification.' Which document would specify that confidential data must be encrypted with AES-256 at rest?",["A guideline","A procedure","A standard","A policy"],2,"Standards are mandatory, specific requirements that support policies, such as a required algorithm or setting. Policies state intent, procedures give steps, and guidelines give optional advice.","Job practice 3A",["Guidelines are recommendations, not mandatory requirements, so a required algorithm would not be placed in one.","Procedures give step-by-step instructions for doing a task, not the mandatory technical requirement itself.",null,"The policy is the high-level statement of intent; specific algorithms belong in a standard so policies stay stable."],1],["cm38",0,3,"Who should approve the organization's information security policy?",["Senior management or the board","The information security manager","The IT help desk manager","An external consultant"],0,"Policies express management's intent and direction, so they must be approved by senior management or the board to carry authority across the organization.","Job practice 3A",[null,"The security manager drafts and maintains the policy but needs senior approval so it carries organizational authority.","The help desk manager has no authority to set organization-wide direction.","Consultants can advise or draft, but they cannot give the policy management's authority."],1],["cm39",0,3,"How often should information security policies be reviewed?",["Only when a regulator formally requests changes to them","Every month by the entire workforce","Never, once they have been approved by the board","At planned intervals and when significant changes occur"],3,"Policies should be reviewed on a schedule, commonly annually, and also after major changes in the business, law or threat environment, so they remain relevant.","Job practice 3A",["Waiting for a regulator means policies can drift out of date for years.","Monthly full-workforce review is impractical and not what policy governance requires.","Approved policies still age; unreviewed policies become outdated and ignored.",null],1],["cm40",0,3,"Which metric is MOST useful to senior management for judging the effectiveness of the security program?",["The number of spam emails blocked each month","Risk trends for critical business processes versus appetite","The count of firewall rule changes made during the last quarter","The average CPU usage of the security servers"],1,"Executives need outcome-based metrics tied to business objectives. Trends in risk to critical processes show whether the program is achieving its purpose.","Job practice 3A",["Blocked spam counts are operational and do not show whether business risk is being managed.",null,"Rule changes measure activity, not effectiveness or business impact.","System performance data is irrelevant to judging program effectiveness."],2],["cm41",0,3,"What is the difference between a key performance indicator (KPI) and a key risk indicator (KRI)?",["A KPI measures performance; a KRI signals changing risk exposure","A KPI is always financial, while a KRI is always a technical measure","KPIs are for auditors and KRIs are for engineers","There is no difference between them in practice"],0,"KPIs show whether a process or control meets its performance targets. KRIs are early warning signs that risk is increasing. Both are useful, but they answer different questions.","Job practice 3A",[null,"Both can be financial or technical; the difference is what they measure, not the type of data.","Both are management tools used by various stakeholders.","Mixing them up causes reporting that shows activity but hides rising risk."],1],["cm42",0,3,"An organization wants to measure the maturity of its security processes over time. Which approach is BEST?",["Count the number of security tools deployed each year","Measure how many hours the security team works","Compare the security budget with the figure from the previous year","Assess against a maturity model and track the levels over time"],3,"Maturity models describe levels from ad hoc to optimized. Assessing against one gives a baseline and a way to show improvement over time.","Job practice 3A",["Tool counts do not measure process maturity.","Hours worked is an effort measure, not maturity.","Budget change does not indicate whether processes are more defined or managed.",null],2],["cm43",0,3,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of an enterprise architecture from a security perspective?",["To produce detailed network diagrams that help engineers troubleshoot outages","To replace the need for risk assessments","To select a single vendor for all technology purchases","To build security consistently into how business and technology fit together"],3,"Enterprise architecture describes how business, information, applications and technology relate. Integrating security architecture into it means controls are designed in consistently rather than added piece by piece.","Job practice 3A",["Troubleshooting diagrams are operational artifacts, not the purpose of enterprise architecture.","Architecture uses risk assessment results; it does not replace them.","Enterprise architecture guides consistency but does not require a single vendor.",null],2],["cm44",0,3,"A security architecture principle states that users and devices must be verified on every access request, regardless of network location. Which model does this describe?",["Defense in depth","Perimeter security","Zero trust","Security through obscurity"],2,"Zero trust assumes no implicit trust based on network location and requires continuous verification of identity, device and context for each access request.","Job practice 3A",["Defense in depth layers multiple controls, but it does not specifically require verifying every request regardless of location.","Perimeter security trusts traffic inside the network boundary, which is the opposite of this principle.",null,"Obscurity relies on hiding details, not verifying access requests."],1],["cm45",0,3,"Which is the MOST important resource consideration when staffing an information security program?",["Having the skills the strategy needs, in-house or sourced","Hiring as many security analysts as the budget allows","Making sure every staff member holds the same certification","Placing all security staff within the IT department"],0,"Program resources must match the strategy. The question is whether the needed skills are available, through hiring, training or outsourcing, not simply headcount.","Job practice 3A",[null,"Headcount without the right skills does not deliver the strategy.","Identical certifications do not cover the range of skills a program needs.","Reporting structure matters, but it is not the main resource consideration and can create conflicts of interest."],2],["cm46",0,3,"Why might an organization adopt a recognized control framework such as ISO/IEC 27002 or NIST SP 800-53?",["To guarantee that no breaches will occur","To avoid having to perform any risk assessment of its own environment","To use a proven set of controls tailored to its own risks","To transfer liability for incidents to the framework's publisher"],2,"Recognized frameworks provide a tested catalog of controls and a common language. Organizations select and tailor controls based on their risk assessment rather than inventing everything.","Job practice 3A",["No framework guarantees security; frameworks reduce risk when properly implemented.","Frameworks expect risk assessment to drive control selection and tailoring.",null,"Using a framework does not transfer any liability."],2],["cm47",0,3,"A control is designed to find unauthorized changes after they occur. Which type of control is it?",["Preventive","Detective","Deterrent","Corrective"],1,"Detective controls identify events after or while they occur, such as log reviews and file integrity monitoring. Preventive controls stop events, and corrective controls fix their effects.","Job practice 3B",["Preventive controls stop an event from happening; this control finds changes that have already happened.",null,"Deterrent controls discourage attempts, for example warning banners; they do not find changes.","Corrective controls restore or fix after an event; finding the change is detection."],1],["cm48",0,3,"A legacy system cannot support multifactor authentication, so the organization restricts it to a jump host that requires MFA and records sessions. What type of control is this?",["A directive control","A compensating control","A deterrent control","A recovery control"],1,"A compensating control is an alternative that meets the intent of a required control when the primary control cannot be implemented. The jump host provides equivalent protection.","Job practice 3B",["Directive controls tell people what to do, such as policies; this is a technical alternative.",null,"The jump host does more than discourage attempts; it enforces MFA and monitoring in place of the missing control.","Recovery controls restore operations after an incident, not protect access in the first place."],3],["cm49",0,3,"What should drive the selection of security controls for a new business application?",["The risk assessment and the control objectives derived from it","The controls that were used by the application's previous version","The preferences of the development team","The cheapest controls on the market"],0,"Controls should be selected to meet control objectives that address assessed risks, within cost and business constraints.","Job practice 3B",[null,"The previous version may have had different risks and gaps.","Developer preference is not a basis for meeting risk-based control objectives.","Cheapest may not meet the objectives; cost matters but after effectiveness."],2],["cm50",0,3,"A new security control is ready to deploy in production. What should happen BEFORE it goes live?",["It should go through change management, including testing and approval","It should be announced in a press release to customers","It should be deployed directly by the security team to save valuable time","It should be reviewed only after the next annual audit"],0,"Change management ensures a control is tested, approved and scheduled so it does not disrupt operations or create new risk.","Job practice 3B",[null,"Customer announcements are not a prerequisite and may reveal security details.","Bypassing change management risks outages and undermines the control environment.","Waiting for audit means untested changes run in production for months."],2],["cm51",0,3,"Which activity BEST confirms that a control is operating effectively?",["Reading the control's design documentation and approved narrative","Testing it against its objective using evidence over time","Asking the control owner whether it works","Checking that the control was purchased from a leading vendor"],1,"Operating effectiveness is shown by testing whether the control consistently achieves its objective, using evidence such as samples over time.","Job practice 3B",["Documentation shows design, not whether the control works in practice.",null,"Inquiry alone is weak evidence and needs corroboration.","Vendor reputation does not prove the control operates effectively here."],2],["cm52",0,3,"What is the MAIN goal of a security awareness program?",["To make sure everyone passes a yearly quiz","To teach every employee how to configure and maintain firewalls","To change behavior so people act securely in their work","To shift liability for incidents onto employees"],2,"Awareness programs aim to change behavior, not just share information. Success is measured by outcomes such as fewer risky actions and more reporting.","Job practice 3B",["Quiz completion is an activity measure and may not reflect behavior.","Technical configuration is specialist training, not general awareness.",null,"Awareness exists to reduce risk, not to assign blame."],1],["cm53",0,3,"Which metric BEST shows that a phishing awareness program is working?",["Simulated phishing reports rising while clicks fall","The number of training modules assigned to each employee","The length of the training videos","The number of employees on the email list"],0,"Rising reports and falling clicks show real behavior change: employees recognize and report phishing rather than falling for it.","Job practice 3B",[null,"Assignments measure activity, not behavior.","Video length says nothing about outcomes.","List size is unrelated to training effectiveness."],2],["cm54",0,3,"Before signing a contract with a cloud service provider, what should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Perform a penetration test against the provider's production systems","Assess the provider's controls against its requirements","Migrate a copy of production data to evaluate performance","Accept the provider's standard terms to speed up onboarding"],1,"Due diligence comes before commitment: assess whether the provider meets security, legal and business requirements, and use results to shape contract terms.","Job practice 3B",["Testing a provider's systems without permission may be illegal and breach terms.",null,"Moving real data before assessment exposes it to unassessed risk.","Accepting standard terms without review may leave security requirements unaddressed."],2],["cm55",0,3,"Which contract clause is MOST important to include when outsourcing processing of sensitive data?",["The right to audit or obtain independent assurance reports","A clause naming the provider's marketing contact","A requirement to use the organization's logo on reports","A promise that the provider will never have a security incident"],0,"Right-to-audit or independent assurance (such as SOC 2 reports) lets the organization verify that the provider maintains agreed controls over time.","Job practice 3B",[null,"A marketing contact does nothing to protect sensitive data.","Branding requirements have no security value.","No provider can guarantee zero incidents; notification and response obligations matter instead."],1],["cm56",0,3,"A key supplier uses a subcontractor to host the organization's data. What risk does this introduce?",["Insider risk from the organization's own employees","Physical risk from the organization's own data center","Fourth-party risk from a party it has no contract with","No additional risk because the supplier signed the contract"],2,"Fourth parties are vendors of your vendors. The organization depends on their controls but lacks direct contractual leverage, so contracts should require flow-down obligations and disclosure.","Job practice 3B",["The subcontractor is external, so this is not insider risk.","The data is hosted by the subcontractor, not the organization's own facility.",null,"The supplier's contract does not automatically bind the subcontractor or ensure its controls."],3],["cm57",0,3,"What is the BEST way to monitor a vendor's security posture throughout the contract?",["Rely on the detailed security assessment completed before signing","Wait for the vendor to report any problems","Regularly review assurance reports, SLA results and metrics","Check the vendor's website for news of breaches"],2,"Ongoing monitoring with regular assurance reports, SLA and metric reviews catches changes in the vendor's posture after onboarding.","Job practice 3B",["A one-time assessment becomes outdated as the vendor changes.","Waiting passively leaves the organization unaware of issues the vendor does not report.",null,"Public news is late and incomplete; it is not systematic monitoring."],2],["cm58",0,3,"When reporting program status to executives, what is the BEST approach?",["Provide raw log data so executives can review it themselves","Use the same detailed technical report for all audiences","Tailor it to the audience and link it to business risk","Report only when a serious incident has happened"],2,"Effective communication matches the audience. Executives need concise information on how the program supports objectives and manages risk.","Job practice 3B",["Raw logs are not meaningful to executives and waste their time.","One report for all audiences is either too technical for executives or too shallow for engineers.",null,"Reporting only after incidents means leaders cannot guide the program."],2],["cm59",0,3,"A business unit has deployed an unapproved cloud file-sharing service. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Block the service at the firewall immediately","Discipline the employees who began using it","Assess the risk of the service and the data it holds","Approve the service quickly to avoid conflict with the business"],2,"Before acting, understand the risk: what data is involved and what controls exist. That guides whether to block, approve with controls or migrate.","Job practice 3B",["Blocking without assessment may disrupt business and push users to other unapproved tools.","Discipline does not address the risk and ignores the business need behind the choice.",null,"Approving without assessment accepts unknown risk."],3],["cm60",0,3,"What is the PRIMARY reason for integrating security into the system development life cycle (SDLC)?",["It removes the need for security testing before each release","Security built in early is more effective and cheaper","It allows developers to skip code reviews","It moves accountability for security to developers"],1,"Security requirements defined early are cheaper to implement and produce stronger designs than fixes after deployment.","Job practice 3B",["Security in the SDLC includes testing; it does not remove it.",null,"Code reviews remain part of a secure SDLC.","Accountability for risk stays with owners and management."],2],["cm61",0,3,"Which is the BEST reason to perform control self-assessments in addition to independent audits?",["They replace independent audits entirely","They build ownership and find issues between audits","They guarantee that controls are always operating effectively","They are required by every regulation"],1,"Control self-assessment involves the people who operate controls and gives more frequent insight, while audits provide independence.","Job practice 3B",["Self-assessments lack independence and cannot replace audits.",null,"No assessment guarantees effectiveness; they provide evidence at a point in time.","Not every regulation requires them; their value is ownership and frequent insight."],3],["cm62",0,3,"Which is the MOST appropriate reporting line for the information security manager to avoid conflicts of interest?",["The network operations manager","A senior executive outside of IT operations","The help desk supervisor","The lead software developer for the core business applications"],1,"Reporting outside IT operations helps the security function raise risks independently and avoid being overruled when security conflicts with IT delivery goals.","Job practice 3A",["Reporting to an operational manager creates a conflict: security findings about that team's work may be suppressed.",null,"The help desk lacks the authority and scope to support an enterprise program.","Development priorities can conflict with security, and the role lacks enterprise authority."],1],["cm63",0,3,"Which is the BEST way to ensure new security requirements are considered in purchasing decisions?",["Ask vendors to state that their products are secure","Let each department decide whether security review is needed","Make security review a required procurement step","Review purchases after they have been deployed"],2,"Embedding security review in procurement makes it a standard checkpoint, so requirements are applied before commitments are made.","Job practice 3B",["Self-declarations without review give little assurance.","Voluntary review leads to inconsistent coverage.",null,"Reviewing after deployment is too late to shape requirements or contracts."],2],["cm64",0,3,"What should the information security manager do when business managers regularly bypass a security standard because it slows down their work?",["Report the managers to the board and request disciplinary action","Remove the standard entirely","Increase the monitoring of the managers' activities","Work with them to meet the control objective with less friction"],3,"Repeated bypassing signals a mismatch between the control and business needs. Working with the business to redesign the control keeps risk managed and improves compliance.","Job practice 3A",["Escalating without understanding the cause damages relationships and doesn't fix the design.","Removing it abandons the control objective and leaves risk unmanaged.","More monitoring doesn't address why the control is impractical.",null],3],["cm65",0,4,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of an incident response plan?",["To identify the attacker so that they can be prosecuted later","To prevent all incidents from occurring","To document the organization's firewall rules","To enable a timely, coordinated response that limits impact"],3,"An incident response plan defines roles, procedures and communications so that the organization can respond quickly and consistently to reduce harm.","Job practice 4A",["Attribution may happen, but the main goal is limiting impact.","Prevention is the job of preventive controls; the plan addresses incidents that occur.","Firewall rules are configuration documentation, not incident response.",null],1],["cm66",0,4,"What must be completed BEFORE recovery time objectives (RTOs) can be set for business processes?",["A penetration test","A disaster recovery site contract","A tabletop exercise","A business impact analysis"],3,"The BIA identifies critical processes and the impact of their disruption over time, which determines the maximum tolerable downtime and the RTOs.","Job practice 4A",["Penetration tests find vulnerabilities; they do not measure business impact of downtime.","Site selection depends on RTOs, so it comes after the BIA.","Exercises test plans that are built from BIA results; they come later.",null],1],["cm67",0,4,"A process can tolerate at most 8 hours of downtime and 1 hour of data loss. Which statement is correct?",["The RPO is 8 hours and the RTO is 1 hour","Both the RTO and RPO are 8 hours","The RTO must be at least 8 hours so that staff are not rushed","RTO must be 8 hours or less and RPO 1 hour or less"],3,"The recovery time objective (RTO) is the target time to restore the process, which must not exceed tolerable downtime. The recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time.","Job practice 4A",["This reverses the terms: RPO measures data loss, RTO measures downtime.","The data loss tolerance is 1 hour, so the RPO cannot be 8 hours.","RTO is a maximum target; it must be 8 hours or less, not at least 8 hours.",null],3],["cm68",0,4,"What is the MAIN difference between a business continuity plan (BCP) and a disaster recovery plan (DRP)?",["A BCP covers only natural disasters; a DRP covers cyberattacks","A DRP is written by the board; a BCP is written by IT","They are two names for the same document","A BCP sustains business functions; a DRP restores IT systems"],3,"The BCP focuses on sustaining business operations during disruption. The DRP is typically IT-focused and restores technology that those operations depend on.","Job practice 4A",["Both plans address many disruption types, not specific causes.","Both are developed by teams across the organization and approved by management.","They are different plans with different scopes that must work together.",null],1],["cm69",0,4,"Which recovery site option offers the fastest recovery at the highest cost?",["Cold site","Warm site","Reciprocal agreement","Hot site"],3,"A hot site is fully equipped with current systems and data and can take over within hours or less. That readiness makes it the most expensive option.","Job practice 4A",["A cold site provides space and utilities only; equipment must be brought in, so recovery takes longer.","A warm site has some equipment but needs data and configuration before use, so recovery is slower than a hot site.","Reciprocal agreements are cheap but unreliable and slow to activate.",null],1],["cm70",0,4,"Why is it important to define incident severity levels in advance?",["So that minor incidents can be ignored","So that attackers know how the organization will respond","So auditors can assign blame for each incident","So incidents are prioritized and escalated consistently"],3,"Predefined severity levels tell responders how to prioritize, whom to escalate to and which resources to engage, reducing delay and inconsistency.","Job practice 4A",["Low-severity incidents are still handled, just with less urgency.","Severity definitions are internal and are not meant for attackers.","Severity supports response, not assigning blame.",null],1],["cm71",0,4,"Which test of an incident response plan has team members discuss their actions in a simulated scenario without affecting systems?",["Full interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Penetration test"],1,"In a tabletop exercise, participants walk through a scenario in discussion to validate roles, decisions and communications without touching production systems.","Job practice 4A",["A full interruption test actually shuts down primary operations and is the most disruptive.",null,"A parallel test runs recovery systems alongside production, which does involve systems.","A penetration test tests technical security, not the response plan."],1],["cm72",0,4,"Who should be included on the incident response team in addition to technical staff?",["Only members of the security operations center","Legal, communications, human resources and business leaders","External journalists to document the response","All employees who work in the affected department or at the site"],1,"Incidents affect legal obligations, public messaging, employees and operations, so a cross-functional team is needed for sound decisions.","Job practice 4A",["Technical staff alone cannot make legal, communications or business decisions.",null,"Journalists are not part of the response team; communications staff handle media.","Including everyone adds confusion; the team should be defined and trained."],1],["cm73",0,4,"How often should the incident response plan be tested?",["Regularly and after significant changes","Only when an incident actually occurs","Once, when the plan is first written","Only when auditors request evidence of testing"],0,"Regular testing, plus testing after major changes, keeps the plan accurate and the team ready.","Job practice 4A",[null,"Waiting for a real incident means discovering plan flaws during a crisis.","A plan tested once becomes outdated as the environment changes.","Testing only for audits leaves gaps in readiness."],1],["cm74",0,4,"A SIEM raises an alert that a user logged in from two countries within ten minutes. What should the analyst do FIRST?",["Validate whether the alert is a real incident","Disable every account in the organization","Notify regulators of a data breach","Reimage the user's workstation to remove any threat"],0,"Triage comes first: confirm whether the alert represents a real incident (for example, a VPN could explain it) before escalating or containing.","Job practice 4B",[null,"Disabling all accounts is a drastic overreaction to one unverified alert.","No breach has been confirmed; notification comes after investigation.","Reimaging destroys evidence and may be unnecessary if the alert is benign."],1],["cm75",0,4,"What is the PRIMARY benefit of security orchestration, automation and response (SOAR) tools?",["Replacing the need for trained incident response staff entirely","Guaranteeing that no incidents will occur","Replacing log collection and correlation","Automating routine response steps for speed and consistency"],3,"SOAR tools run playbooks that automate routine tasks such as enrichment and ticketing, which speeds response and reduces human error.","Job practice 4B",["Automation supports analysts; decisions and complex analysis still need people.","SOAR improves response; it does not prevent incidents.","Log collection and correlation are SIEM functions that SOAR relies on.",null],2],["cm76",0,4,"During an investigation, why is maintaining chain of custody important?",["It makes the investigation go faster","It shows evidence was handled properly, so it can be relied on","It allows evidence to be shared publicly","It replaces the need to calculate hashes of the collected evidence"],1,"Chain of custody records who handled evidence, when and how. Without it, evidence may be challenged as altered and be inadmissible.","Job practice 4B",["Chain of custody adds documentation; its value is integrity, not speed.",null,"Chain of custody controls access; it does not permit public sharing.","Hashing complements chain of custody by proving integrity; both are used."],1],["cm77",0,4,"Ransomware is spreading on a network segment. What should the incident response team do FIRST?",["Restore all systems from backup immediately","Pay the ransom to recover the data quickly","Hold a lessons-learned meeting with all affected teams","Contain the spread by isolating the affected systems"],3,"Containment limits damage. Isolating infected systems stops spread while preserving the ability to investigate and recover.","Job practice 4B",["Restoring before containment may reinfect restored systems.","Paying does not stop spread, may be illegal and does not guarantee recovery.","Lessons learned happens after the incident is resolved.",null],1],["cm78",0,4,"Which containment decision requires input from business management rather than only the technical team?",["Shutting down a revenue-generating system to stop an attack","Blocking a known malicious IP address at the perimeter firewall","Quarantining a single malware file on one workstation","Resetting the password of one compromised account"],0,"Containment actions that cause business disruption involve trade-offs between security and operations, so business owners must be involved in the decision, ideally with authority pre-defined in the plan.","Job practice 4B",[null,"Blocking a malicious IP has minimal business impact and is usually pre-authorized.","Routine quarantine is a technical action with limited business impact.","Resetting a compromised account is standard and usually delegated to technical staff."],3],["cm79",0,4,"After an attacker is removed from a network, what is the MAIN goal of the eradication phase?",["Notify all customers of the incident","Restore normal business operations","Remove the root cause and every trace of the attacker","Identify who was responsible for the security failure"],2,"Eradication removes malware, backdoors, compromised accounts and the exploited weakness, so recovery does not reintroduce the threat.","Job practice 4B",["Notification is a communications activity with its own triggers, not eradication.","Restoring operations is the recovery phase, which follows eradication.",null,"Blame is not the goal; the root cause of the technical failure is."],1],["cm80",0,4,"Before restoring systems from backup after a ransomware attack, what should the team verify?",["That the backup vendor has been paid for the current year","That all employees have completed awareness training","The backups are clean and the vulnerability is fixed","That the press has been informed of the restoration"],2,"Restoring infected backups or leaving the entry point open leads to reinfection. The team must verify backup integrity and confirm the exploited weakness is closed.","Job practice 4B",["Vendor billing is unrelated to safe restoration.","Training is valuable but not a precondition for technical restoration.",null,"Media communication is handled separately and is not a restoration prerequisite."],2],["cm81",0,4,"Who should normally decide when and how to notify customers and regulators of a data breach?",["Senior management, advised by legal counsel","The analyst who first detected the breach","The external penetration testing firm under contract","Any employee who knows about the breach"],0,"Breach notification has legal and reputational consequences. Senior management decides with legal advice, following predefined procedures and regulatory deadlines.","Job practice 4B",[null,"Front-line analysts lack the authority and legal knowledge for notification decisions.","An outside testing firm has no authority over the organization's legal obligations.","Uncoordinated disclosure creates legal risk and inconsistent messages."],1],["cm82",0,4,"Why should escalation paths and contact lists be documented before an incident?",["So the right people are reached quickly during an incident","So auditors have fewer questions to ask","So that incidents are handled only by senior executives","So that employees no longer need to report incidents themselves"],0,"Predefined escalation paths save time and prevent mistakes. During a crisis, teams should not be searching for who to call.","Job practice 4B",[null,"Audit is not the main reason; readiness is.","Escalation paths route incidents to the right level, not only to executives.","Employees must still report; escalation paths define what happens after."],1],["cm83",0,4,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of a post-incident review?",["To decide on disciplinary action for the people who were involved","To close the incident ticket as quickly as possible","To calculate the insurance claim amount","To identify improvements to controls and the response process"],3,"Lessons learned find what worked, what didn't and what to change so that the organization improves and similar incidents are less likely or less harmful.","Job practice 4B",["A blame-focused review discourages honest input and misses systemic causes.","Closing tickets is administrative; the review's value is improvement.","Cost data may be gathered, but improvement is the main purpose.",null],1],["cm84",0,4,"An investigation finds that the same type of incident has happened three times this year. What should the information security manager do?",["Find the root cause and fix it through updated controls","Close each incident as soon as the affected systems are restored","Hire additional analysts to respond faster next time","Reclassify the incidents as low severity"],0,"Repeated incidents indicate a root cause has not been fixed. Addressing that cause is more effective than repeatedly responding.","Job practice 4B",[null,"Restoring without fixing the cause invites the fourth occurrence.","Faster response helps but does not stop recurrence.","Reclassifying hides the problem instead of solving it."],3],["cm85",0,4,"A forensic analyst needs to examine a compromised server's disk. What should be done to preserve evidence integrity?",["Browse the original disk to look for suspicious files","Create a forensic image, hash it and analyze the copy","Run an antivirus scan on the original disk","Reinstall the operating system and then investigate"],1,"Working on a verified forensic copy preserves the original. Matching hashes prove the copy is identical and has not been altered.","Job practice 4B",["Browsing the original changes access times and other metadata, which can damage evidence.",null,"Scanning modifies files and metadata and may delete evidence.","Reinstalling destroys the evidence that needs to be examined."],1],["cm86",0,4,"What should incident classification criteria be MOST closely based on?",["Business impact, data sensitivity and scope","The type and family of malware that was detected","The time of day the incident was detected","The number of alerts the SIEM generated"],0,"Classification should reflect business impact so that the most significant incidents receive the most urgent attention and resources.","Job practice 4A",[null,"Malware type matters technically, but business impact determines priority.","Detection time does not determine significance.","Alert volume doesn't necessarily reflect impact."],3],["cm87",0,4,"Which is the BEST way to verify that backups can meet the recovery point objective?",["Run regular test restores and check the recovered data's age","Check that the nightly backup jobs report a successful status","Ask the backup administrator for confirmation","Increase the storage capacity for backups"],0,"Only a test restore proves backups work and shows how much data would be lost, confirming whether the RPO is met.","Job practice 4A",[null,"A success message doesn't prove data can be restored.","Verbal confirmation isn't evidence.","More capacity doesn't prove recoverability or backup frequency."],2],["cm88",0,4,"During a major incident, a journalist calls a help desk employee asking for details. What should the employee do?",["Share what they know to show the organization is being transparent","Refer the caller to the designated communications contact","Deny that any incident is occurring","Hang up without saying anything"],1,"Only designated spokespeople should communicate externally during an incident, so messages are accurate, consistent and legally reviewed.","Job practice 4B",["Unauthorized disclosure may be inaccurate and create legal exposure.",null,"Denying a real incident is dishonest and damages trust when the truth emerges.","Hanging up is unprofessional and doesn't direct the inquiry to the right person."],2],["cm89",0,4,"What is the BEST reason to align the incident response plan with the business continuity plan?",["So that only one team is needed to handle every kind of emergency","So the incident response plan can be deleted","So auditors can review a single document","So an escalating incident triggers continuity actions smoothly"],3,"Incidents can become disruptions. Aligned plans make handoffs clear so that continuity measures start without delay.","Job practice 4A",["Different teams are still needed; alignment coordinates them.","Both plans remain necessary.","Audit convenience is not the purpose; operational coordination is.",null],3],["cm90",0,4,"Which metric is MOST useful for evaluating incident response performance over time?",["The number of firewalls in the network","The number of pages in the response plan","Mean time to detect and mean time to contain","The number of vendors who attend each post-incident review"],2,"Time to detect and time to contain show how quickly the organization finds and limits incidents, which directly relates to impact.","Job practice 4B",["Device counts don't measure response performance.","Plan length doesn't indicate how well it works.",null,"Vendor attendance is unrelated to response performance."],1],["x1",0,1,"A board member asks why the company should fund a security program when no breach has occurred in five years. What is the BEST response from the information security manager?",["Show a list of every security tool the company currently uses","State that industry peers spend more on security than the company does according to a recent survey","Explain the risks to business objectives and how the program keeps them within appetite","Promise that funding the program will guarantee no breach ever occurs"],2,"The board needs to understand security in terms of business risk and how investment keeps that risk within the agreed appetite. A past lack of breaches does not mean the risk is low, and no program can guarantee zero breaches.","Job practice 1: Business case for security",["A tool inventory describes what is owned, not why it matters to the business.","Peer spending is a weak argument on its own because each organization's risk profile is different.",null,"No program can guarantee that breaches will never happen, and promising it damages credibility."],2],["x2",0,1,"Two business units interpret the same data protection standard differently, causing inconsistent controls. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Clarify the standard's intent with stakeholders and issue guidance so it is applied consistently","Let each unit keep its own interpretation because both are compliant in their view and nothing has gone wrong","Audit both units and report the one with weaker controls to the board","Rewrite the standard in more technical terms so there is less room for opinion"],0,"Inconsistent interpretation is a governance problem; the manager should confirm the intended requirement with stakeholders and publish guidance so controls are applied uniformly. Escalating one unit to the board before clarifying the standard treats a clarity issue as misconduct.","Job practice 1: Policies and standards",[null,"Two conflicting interpretations mean at least one may not meet the standard's intent, so leaving them is risky.","Reporting a unit before clarifying the requirement is premature when the standard itself is unclear.","More technical wording often makes a standard harder for business units to apply, not easier."],3],["x3",0,1,"Which document sets the high-level direction and management intent for information security across the organization?",["A firewall configuration standard","A server hardening procedure","A password complexity guideline","The information security policy"],3,"The information security policy states management's intent and direction at a high level; standards, procedures and guidelines support it with more detail. A firewall standard covers one specific technical area.","Job practice 1: Policy framework",["A firewall standard sets detailed technical rules for one area, not organization-wide direction.","A hardening procedure gives step-by-step instructions, not management intent.","A guideline is advisory and supports policy rather than setting direction.",null],1],["x4",0,1,"An information security manager is asked to report to the head of IT operations, who is measured on system uptime. What is the MAIN concern with this reporting line?",["The head of IT operations may lack budget for security tools this year","Security decisions may be overridden to protect uptime targets","Security staff will have to learn new operations terminology","Uptime reports will need to include security metrics as well"],1,"When security reports to someone measured mainly on availability, there is a conflict of interest that can lead to security needs being deprioritized. Budget constraints may follow, but the root issue is the conflict of objectives.","Job practice 1: Organizational structure",["Budget could be a symptom, but the main issue is that security goals conflict with the manager's targets.",null,"Learning terminology is a minor adjustment, not a governance concern.","Adding metrics to reports is not a risk; the conflict of interest is."],2],["x5",0,1,"An organization is adopting a new cloud-first strategy. How should the information security strategy respond?",["Keep the existing strategy because security principles never change","Update security objectives, architecture and controls to support the new business direction","Block cloud adoption until the current on-premises controls are fully mature and fully documented","Create a separate cloud security strategy owned only by the cloud team"],1,"The security strategy must stay aligned with business strategy; a shift to cloud requires updating objectives, architecture and controls. Blocking the business direction until old controls mature puts security at odds with the organization's goals.","Job practice 1: Strategy alignment",["Principles may persist, but objectives and controls must change when the business model changes.",null,"Blocking a business strategy positions security as an obstacle rather than an enabler of objectives.","A separate strategy owned by one team fragments governance and risks inconsistent controls."],3],["x6",0,1,"What is the PRIMARY role of senior management in information security governance?",["Configuring security tools and reviewing daily alerts","Performing technical audits of security controls","Writing detailed operating procedures for the security team and its contractors","Setting direction, approving risk appetite and providing resources"],3,"Senior management sets direction, defines acceptable risk and provides the resources and support needed. Configuring tools and reviewing alerts are operational tasks for security staff.","Job practice 1: Roles and responsibilities",["Tool configuration and alert review are operational tasks, not governance duties.","Technical audits are performed by audit or assurance functions, not senior management.","Detailed procedures are written by process owners and the security team.",null],2],["x7",0,1,"A new regulation will require breach notification within 72 hours. The organization's current process takes about a week to confirm a breach. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Perform a gap analysis of the process against the new requirement and plan remediation","Buy a new SIEM to detect incidents faster","Tell the legal team the requirement cannot be met","Wait until the first breach to see how long notification really takes in practice for the company"],0,"A gap analysis identifies where the current detection, triage and escalation process falls short of the 72-hour requirement, so the right changes can be planned. Buying a tool first may not fix the actual bottleneck, which could be decision-making rather than detection.","Job practice 1: Legal and regulatory requirements",[null,"A new tool may not address the real delay, which might lie in escalation or decision-making.","Declaring the requirement impossible without analysis accepts noncompliance by default.","Waiting for a breach means discovering the gap when penalties already apply."],3],["x8",0,1,"Which is the BEST way to build a security-aware culture across the organization?",["A single annual email reminding staff to follow the security policy and its standards","Strict penalties for every minor policy violation","Visible leadership support combined with relevant, role-based messaging","Limiting security information to the IT department"],2,"Culture changes when leaders visibly model and support security and when messages are relevant to people's roles. Harsh penalties for minor slips tend to discourage reporting rather than build a positive culture.","Job practice 1: Organizational culture",["One annual email is easily ignored and does little to change everyday behavior.","Heavy penalties for minor issues can drive mistakes underground and reduce reporting.",null,"Keeping security inside IT prevents the wider workforce from understanding their role."],2],["x9",0,1,"In a RACI chart for security policy approval, what does the 'A' identify?",["The people who carry out the work of drafting the policy and supporting standards","The people who must be asked for their input","The people who are informed after the decision is made","The single role that is ultimately answerable for the outcome"],3,"In RACI, Accountable is the one role that owns the outcome and signs off; Responsible roles do the work. Having one accountable role avoids confusion over who decides.","Job practice 1: Roles and responsibilities",["Doing the work is the Responsible role, not Accountable.","Being asked for input describes the Consulted role.","Being told after the fact describes the Informed role.",null],1],["x10",0,1,"After a merger, the combined company has two security policies with conflicting requirements. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Adopt the stricter policy everywhere immediately without consultation","Keep both policies permanently so neither workforce is disrupted","Assess both against business needs and risk, then propose a single harmonized policy for approval","Adopt the policy of the larger company because it covers more staff"],2,"Harmonizing policies should start from business requirements and risk, with stakeholder input, before seeking management approval for one policy. Imposing the stricter policy without analysis may create unworkable controls and resistance.","Job practice 1: Governance after organizational change",["The stricter policy may not fit all operations and imposing it without analysis invites workarounds.","Two conflicting policies leave gaps and confusion across the combined company.",null,"Company size does not show which policy best fits the combined risk profile."],3],["x11",0,1,"Which is the MOST important input when defining the scope of an information security program?",["The list of security products the team already knows how to use with confidence","The organization's business objectives and critical information assets","The number of security staff currently employed","The scope used by a competitor's program"],1,"The program's scope should cover the assets and processes that matter to business objectives. Existing tools and staff are resources to plan with, not the basis for deciding what needs protection.","Job practice 1: Program scope",["Familiar tools are a resource consideration and should not decide what the program must protect.",null,"Staff numbers affect capacity but do not define what needs protecting.","A competitor's scope reflects their business, not this organization's assets and objectives."],2],["x12",0,2,"A risk owner accepts a high risk that exceeds the organization's stated risk appetite. What should the information security manager do?",["Escalate the decision to the level of management authorized to accept that risk","Record the acceptance and take no further action","Implement additional controls without the owner's agreement","Remove the risk from the register since it has been accepted by the business owner"],0,"Risk above appetite must be accepted by someone with the authority to do so, often senior management or a risk committee. Simply recording it lets a decision stand that the owner was not authorized to make.","Job practice 2: Risk acceptance",[null,"Recording it without escalation lets an unauthorized acceptance stand.","Imposing controls without the owner's agreement bypasses governance and may not be funded.","Accepted risks stay on the register so they can be monitored and revisited."],2],["x13",0,2,"An asset worth $200,000 has an exposure factor of 25% for a given threat, which is expected twice a year. A control costing $60,000 per year would cut the frequency to once every two years. Is the control justified on cost alone?",["No, because expected loss falls by only $37,500 a year, which is less than the $60,000 annual cost for the company","No, because the annual loss expectancy of $50,000 is below the control cost","Yes, because it reduces annual expected loss from $100,000 to $25,000, saving $75,000 against a $60,000 cost","Yes, because any control that lowers frequency is always worth its cost"],2,"SLE is $50,000 (25% of $200,000); ALE before is $100,000 (two per year) and after is $25,000 (0.5 per year), a $75,000 reduction that exceeds the $60,000 cost. Treating $50,000 as the ALE confuses single loss expectancy with the annualized figure.","Job practice 2: Quantitative risk analysis",["The reduction is $75,000 ($100,000 minus $25,000), not $37,500, so it exceeds the control cost.","$50,000 is the single loss expectancy; with two events a year the ALE is $100,000.",null,"Controls must be weighed against their cost; lowering frequency is not automatically worth any price."],3],["x14",0,2,"Which risk response involves stopping the activity that creates the risk?",["Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transfer","Risk acceptance"],0,"Avoidance removes the risk by not doing the activity, such as not launching a risky service. Mitigation keeps the activity but reduces likelihood or impact with controls.","Job practice 2: Risk treatment",[null,"Mitigation keeps the activity and lowers the risk with controls.","Transfer shifts financial impact to another party, such as an insurer, while the activity continues.","Acceptance means continuing the activity and living with the risk."],1],["x15",0,2,"A vulnerability with a high technical severity score is found on an isolated test server holding no real data. How should it be prioritized?",["As the top priority because the technical score is high","Ignored entirely because test servers never matter","Based on how long the vulnerability has been publicly known by vendors and security researchers","Based on business risk, which may be lower because of limited exposure and impact"],3,"Prioritization should consider the asset's value, exposure and potential business impact, not only the technical score. Ignoring it entirely is wrong because test systems can still be used as footholds if connected later.","Job practice 2: Vulnerability and risk prioritization",["A high technical score does not by itself mean high business risk in this context.","Test servers can still become a foothold or be reconnected, so they should not be ignored.","Age of disclosure is one input, but business impact and exposure matter more.",null],2],["x16",0,2,"A third-party risk assessment shows that a key supplier has weak incident response capability. The supplier cannot be replaced for two years. What is the BEST course of action?",["Terminate the contract immediately regardless of business impact","Agree on a remediation plan with the supplier and add compensating controls and monitoring","Accept the risk without documentation because there is no alternative and the supplier cannot be replaced","Ask the supplier to self-certify that its capability has improved"],1,"When a supplier cannot be replaced, the organization should work with it on improvements, add its own compensating controls, and monitor progress, with the residual risk formally accepted. Undocumented acceptance leaves no accountability or follow-up.","Job practice 2: Third-party risk",["Immediate termination could disrupt critical operations more than the risk itself.",null,"Even unavoidable risk must be documented and accepted by the right owner.","Self-certification without evidence gives little assurance that anything changed."],3],["x17",0,2,"What is the MAIN benefit of using threat scenarios in a risk assessment?",["They replace the need to identify vulnerabilities in systems","They link specific threats to assets and business impact so risks can be prioritized","They make the assessment independent of business context","They guarantee that every possible attack has been considered by the attackers targeting the organization"],1,"Scenarios describe how a threat could exploit a weakness to affect an asset and the business, which makes risks concrete and comparable. No method can guarantee that every possible attack is covered.","Job practice 2: Risk scenarios",["Scenarios build on vulnerability information rather than replacing it.",null,"Scenarios are valuable precisely because they connect risk to business context.","No assessment method can cover every possible attack."],2],["x18",0,2,"Key risk indicators show phishing click rates rising for three straight months in the finance department. What should the information security manager do FIRST?",["Wait for the annual risk assessment to reassess the risk and all related controls","Remove the KRI because it is producing bad news","Block all external email for the finance department","Analyze the cause and adjust controls or training for that department"],3,"A KRI trend crossing expectations is an early warning that should trigger analysis and targeted action. Blocking all external email would disrupt the business and is disproportionate before understanding the cause.","Job practice 2: Risk monitoring",["Waiting for the annual cycle defeats the purpose of an early warning indicator.","Removing the indicator hides the risk rather than managing it.","Blocking all external email is disproportionate and would stop normal finance work.",null],3],["x19",0,2,"Which statement BEST describes inherent risk?",["The level of risk before any controls are applied","The risk that remains after controls are applied","The risk that an auditor fails to detect an error during a review","The risk the organization is willing to accept"],0,"Inherent risk is the exposure that exists before considering controls; residual risk is what remains after them. Comparing the two shows how much the controls reduce risk.","Job practice 2: Risk concepts",[null,"Risk remaining after controls is residual risk.","An auditor failing to detect an error is detection risk, an audit concept.","The risk the organization is willing to accept is its risk appetite."],2],["x20",0,2,"The organization is considering a generative AI tool that employees would use with customer data. Which risk should the information security manager assess FIRST?",["Whether the AI tool's user interface is easy to learn","Whether the tool's license is cheaper than competitors offering similar features to the business","Whether customer data could be retained, reused or exposed by the AI provider","Whether employees will enjoy using the new tool"],2,"The primary security concern is how the provider handles customer data, including retention, training use and exposure, since this drives privacy and contractual obligations. Usability and cost are business considerations but not the first risk to assess.","Job practice 2: Emerging risk",["Ease of use affects adoption but not data protection risk.","Cost is a procurement factor, not the key security risk.",null,"Employee enjoyment is unrelated to the risk of exposing customer data."],3],["x21",0,2,"Why should a risk register record the risk owner for each risk?",["So auditors know whom to blame if an incident occurs","So the security team can avoid discussing risks with the business and its day-to-day decisions","So the risk can be deleted when the owner leaves the company","So someone with authority is accountable for decisions and follow-up on the risk"],3,"A named risk owner with appropriate authority ensures treatment decisions are made and monitored. Ownership is about accountability and action, not assigning blame.","Job practice 2: Risk ownership",["Ownership supports accountability and action; it is not meant for assigning blame.","Named owners increase engagement between security and the business rather than avoiding it.","When an owner leaves, the risk is reassigned, not deleted.",null],2],["x22",0,2,"Which term describes the amount of risk an organization is willing to pursue or retain to achieve its objectives?",["Risk exposure","Risk register","Risk appetite","Risk transfer"],2,"Risk appetite is the broad level of risk the organization is willing to accept in pursuit of its goals. Risk exposure is the actual level of risk faced.","Job practice 2: Risk concepts",["Risk exposure describes the actual risk faced, not what is acceptable.","A risk register is the record of risks, not a tolerance level.",null,"Risk transfer is a treatment option, not a willingness level."],1],["x23",0,2,"A risk assessment rated a system's risk as low. Six months later, the system starts processing payment data. What should happen?",["Keep the low rating until the next scheduled annual assessment","Reassess the risk because the change in data affects impact and requirements","Rate it high automatically without analysis","Remove the system from the register because payment data is covered elsewhere"],1,"Significant changes such as new data types should trigger a risk reassessment, because impact and compliance obligations change. Waiting for the annual cycle leaves the risk understated in the meantime.","Job practice 2: Risk reassessment triggers",["Waiting for the annual cycle leaves outdated ratings in use while exposure has grown.",null,"An automatic rating without analysis may be wrong and does not identify needed controls.","Payment data adds requirements to this system; removing it from the register hides the risk."],3],["x24",0,3,"An information security manager is building a security architecture for a new digital platform. Which approach is BEST?",["Derive architecture requirements from business needs, risks and the enterprise architecture","Copy a vendor's reference architecture without changes","Let each development team choose its own security design","Start by buying the most advanced security tools available"],0,"Security architecture should be driven by business requirements and risk and fit within the enterprise architecture so controls are consistent. Copying a vendor's design without tailoring may leave gaps or add unneeded cost.","Job practice 3: Security architecture",[null,"A reference architecture is a starting point but must be tailored to the organization's risks and systems.","Independent team designs create inconsistent controls and integration gaps.","Buying tools before defining requirements leads to poor fit and wasted spending."],2],["x25",0,3,"Which type of control is a security awareness poster reminding staff to lock their screens?",["Corrective","Compensating","Directive","Recovery"],2,"A directive control tells people what to do, guiding behavior through policy or instruction. A corrective control fixes a problem after it happens.","Job practice 3: Control types",["Corrective controls act after an event to fix it, not to guide behavior beforehand.","Compensating controls stand in for a primary control that cannot be used.",null,"Recovery controls restore operations after an incident."],1],["x26",0,3,"A security program's metrics dashboard reports 98% patch compliance, yet a breach occurred through an unpatched internet-facing server. What is the MOST likely problem with the metric?",["It does not weight systems by risk or cover all assets","Patch compliance should be reported as a raw number instead of a percentage","The dashboard is updated too frequently to be accurate","Patch metrics are never useful for security programs"],0,"An overall percentage can hide that the few unpatched systems are the most exposed or that some assets are not in scope. Metrics should be risk-weighted and based on complete asset inventories.","Job practice 3: Program metrics",[null,"Changing the format to raw numbers does not address the missing risk weighting or coverage.","Frequent updates improve timeliness and are not the cause of misleading results.","Patch metrics are useful when designed well; the issue is how this one is built."],3],["x27",0,3,"Which is the BEST reason to classify information assets before selecting controls?",["It allows every asset to receive the same maximum level of protection regardless of its value","It removes the need for a risk assessment","It lets the IT department decide who owns each asset","It ensures protection is proportional to the value and sensitivity of the information"],3,"Classification lets the organization apply stronger controls where information is more sensitive and avoid overspending on low-value data. Applying maximum protection to everything is costly and impractical.","Job practice 3: Information asset classification",["Maximum protection for all assets wastes resources and can hinder the business.","Classification informs risk assessment but does not replace it.","Ownership is assigned to business owners, not decided by IT through classification.",null],2],["x28",0,3,"A business unit requests an exception to the encryption standard for a legacy system that cannot support it. What should the information security manager do?",["Deny the request because standards must never have exceptions","Assess the risk, identify compensating controls, and obtain time-limited approval from the risk owner","Approve the exception permanently to avoid future disputes","Ignore the request until the system is replaced"],1,"A formal exception process evaluates the risk, applies compensating controls and gets approval from the appropriate owner with an expiry date for review. Blanket denial ignores real constraints and may push the business to work around security.","Job practice 3: Exception management",["A no-exceptions stance ignores technical realities and encourages unapproved workarounds.",null,"Permanent exceptions are never revisited and allow risk to grow unnoticed.","Ignoring the request leaves the risk unmanaged and undocumented."],3],["x29",0,3,"When should security requirements be included in a contract with a new cloud service provider?",["After the service has gone live and issues appear with the new service","Before the contract is signed, during negotiation","Only when the contract comes up for renewal","Only if the provider asks for them"],1,"Security requirements such as breach notification, audit rights and data location are easiest to secure during negotiation. After signing, the organization has much less leverage.","Job practice 3: Managing external services",["After go-live, the organization has little leverage and may be exposed in the meantime.",null,"Waiting for renewal leaves the data unprotected by contract for the whole term.","The organization must define its own requirements rather than wait for the provider."],2],["x30",0,3,"Which metric would BEST show the board whether the security program is reducing risk over time?",["Total number of alerts processed by the security operations center each day and escalated to analysts","Number of security policies published this year","Percentage of the IT budget spent on security software","Trend in the number of high risks outside appetite across critical business processes"],3,"Tracking high risks outside appetite over time shows whether the program is bringing risk to acceptable levels, which is what the board cares about. Alert volume is an operational measure with no clear link to risk reduction.","Job practice 3: Reporting to stakeholders",["Alert counts reflect activity levels, not whether business risk is falling.","Publishing policies is an output, not evidence of reduced risk.","Budget share shows spending, not results.",null],2],["x31",0,3,"The security team wants to deploy a new data loss prevention tool in blocking mode on day one. What is the BEST approach?",["Start in monitoring mode, tune rules with business input, then enable blocking in stages","Enable blocking for all users immediately to show strong security to leadership","Deploy only to the security team and never extend it further","Skip change management because it is a security improvement"],0,"Starting in monitoring mode reveals false positives and business impact, so rules can be tuned before blocking; staged rollout limits disruption. Immediate blocking often stops legitimate work and damages trust in the program.","Job practice 3: Control implementation",[null,"Immediate blocking can halt legitimate business processes and create pressure to disable the tool.","Limiting it to the security team leaves the rest of the business unprotected.","Security changes still need change management to avoid unintended outages."],3],["x32",0,3,"What is the MAIN purpose of a security baseline for workstations?",["To list every application employees are allowed to request and install on their workstations","To replace the need for endpoint protection software","To define a minimum secure configuration applied consistently","To record which employee is assigned to each workstation"],2,"A baseline sets the minimum security settings so every workstation starts from a known secure state and drift can be detected. It does not replace other controls such as endpoint protection.","Job practice 3: Standards and baselines",["An application catalog is a different document from a security configuration baseline.","Baselines complement endpoint protection; they do not replace it.",null,"Asset assignment belongs in the inventory, not in a configuration baseline."],2],["x33",0,3,"The security team is small and cannot cover 24/7 monitoring. Management is considering a managed security service provider (MSSP). What should the information security manager ensure?",["The MSSP takes full accountability for all security incidents","The internal team stops reviewing any security alerts","The contract allows the MSSP to change controls without notice or approval from the organization","Roles, escalation, SLAs and the organization's retained accountability are clearly defined"],3,"Outsourcing monitoring can fill a capability gap, but the organization keeps accountability; the contract and processes must define responsibilities, escalation paths and service levels. Accountability for risk cannot be transferred to a provider.","Job practice 3: Program resources and outsourcing",["Accountability for security stays with the organization even when operations are outsourced.","The internal team must still handle escalations and oversee the provider.","Unannounced control changes by a provider undermine change management and oversight.",null],3],["x34",0,3,"Which document provides step-by-step instructions for performing a specific security task?",["A policy","A guideline","A procedure","A charter"],2,"Procedures describe the detailed steps to carry out a task, such as provisioning a user. Policies state high-level intent and guidelines offer optional advice.","Job practice 3: Policies, standards, procedures and guidelines",["A policy sets high-level intent, not detailed steps.","A guideline offers recommendations that are not mandatory step-by-step instructions.",null,"A charter establishes a function's authority and mandate, not task steps."],1],["x35",0,3,"Control testing shows that a quarterly access review is performed, but reviewers approve all access without changes. What does this MOST likely indicate?",["The control is highly effective because no issues were found","The control may be operating in form but not in substance","The access review should be performed less often","The reviewers have too little access themselves"],1,"Blanket approvals suggest reviewers are not actually evaluating access, so the control exists on paper but may not be effective. Assuming zero changes means perfect access is unlikely in a changing organization.","Job practice 3: Control testing and evaluation",["Zero changes over many cycles is unlikely in practice and suggests rubber-stamping rather than effectiveness.",null,"Reducing frequency would weaken a control that already seems ineffective.","Reviewer access levels are not the issue; the quality of review is."],2],["x36",0,3,"A company uses a SaaS provider that relies on another cloud provider for hosting. How should the information security manager address fourth-party risk?",["Require the SaaS provider to disclose key subcontractors and manage their security through contract terms","Contact the hosting provider directly and demand audit rights","Ignore it because the organization has no contract with the hosting provider and the risk sits with the SaaS provider","Require the SaaS provider to stop using any subcontractors"],0,"The organization manages fourth-party risk through its direct supplier, requiring disclosure of critical subcontractors and flow-down of security obligations. It usually has no contractual standing to demand audit rights from the hosting provider directly.","Job practice 3: Third- and fourth-party risk",[null,"Without a contract, the organization typically cannot demand audit rights from a fourth party.","Lack of a direct contract does not remove the risk to the organization's data.","Banning subcontractors is unrealistic for most cloud services and may not be possible."],3],["x37",0,3,"Which is the BEST way to make security awareness training more effective for developers?",["Give developers the same generic training used for all staff","Replace training with a signed statement that they understand security and the acceptable use policy","Provide role-specific training on secure coding with practical examples","Train developers only after a security incident involving their code"],2,"Role-based training targets the risks developers actually create and manage, such as insecure code patterns. Generic training covers basics but not the specific skills developers need.","Job practice 3: Security awareness and training",["Generic content misses the specific secure coding practices developers need.","A signed statement does not build skills or change behavior.",null,"Training only after incidents is reactive and allows avoidable flaws to reach production."],2],["x38",0,3,"Budget cuts force the security program to drop one of four planned initiatives. How should the information security manager decide?",["Choose based on which initiative least reduces risk to critical business objectives","Drop the initiative that the security team finds least interesting and least exciting to work on","Drop the most expensive initiative regardless of its risk benefit","Ask each vendor which of their products is most important"],0,"Prioritization should compare how much each initiative reduces risk to what matters most to the business, relative to its cost. Cutting the most expensive item may remove the one that addresses the biggest risk.","Job practice 3: Program resource prioritization",[null,"Team preference is not a sound basis for deciding how to reduce business risk.","Cost alone ignores the value each initiative delivers in risk reduction.","Vendors have a sales interest and cannot judge the organization's risk priorities."],3],["x39",0,3,"Which control framework is published by the Center for Internet Security as a prioritized set of safeguards?",["ISO/IEC 27001","COBIT","ITIL","CIS Critical Security Controls"],3,"The CIS Critical Security Controls are a prioritized set of safeguards grouped into implementation groups. ISO/IEC 27001 defines requirements for an information security management system.","Job practice 3: Standards and frameworks",["ISO/IEC 27001 is an ISMS requirements standard from ISO and IEC, not CIS.","COBIT is ISACA's framework for governance and management of enterprise IT.","ITIL is a framework for IT service management practices.",null],2],["x40",0,3,"A development team wants to move fast with a new mobile app and asks security to review it only at the end. What is the BEST response?",["Agree to a single review at the end to avoid slowing the team","Integrate security activities such as threat modeling and automated testing throughout development","Refuse to support the project until a full waterfall process is adopted","Assign the security team to write the app's code"],1,"Building security into each phase finds issues early, when they are cheaper to fix, and fits fast-moving teams through automation. A single late review often finds problems too late to fix before launch.","Job practice 3: Security in development",["A single late review finds issues when fixes are costly and schedules are fixed.",null,"Demanding a different methodology puts security at odds with the business instead of adapting to it.","Security writing the code blurs responsibilities and does not scale."],3],["x41",0,3,"What is the PRIMARY goal of an information security program?",["To buy the latest security technology each year","To protect information assets in line with business objectives and risk appetite","To eliminate all risk from the organization","To pass external audits with no findings"],1,"A security program exists to protect information assets in a way that supports business objectives and keeps risk within appetite. Eliminating all risk is impossible and would stop the business from operating.","Job practice 3: Program objectives",["Buying technology is a means, not the goal of the program.",null,"Eliminating all risk is neither possible nor affordable.","Passing audits is a byproduct of good security, not its main purpose."],1],["x42",0,3,"An organization is implementing a zero trust architecture. Which change is MOST consistent with that model?",["Trusting all devices once they are connected to the internal network without further checks","Removing multifactor authentication for users inside the office","Using a single perimeter firewall as the main security control","Granting access per request based on verified identity, device health and context"],3,"Zero trust removes implicit trust based on network location and evaluates each access request using identity, device posture and context. Trusting devices because they are on the internal network is the perimeter model zero trust replaces.","Job practice 3: Security architecture",["Trusting the internal network is the implicit trust that zero trust eliminates.","Removing MFA inside the office relies on location-based trust, which zero trust rejects.","A single perimeter firewall reflects the traditional model, not zero trust.",null],2],["x43",0,3,"A security manager needs to present program results to three audiences: the board, IT managers and the SOC team. What is the BEST approach?",["Tailor the level of detail and metrics to each audience's decisions","Send the same detailed technical report to all three groups","Present only to the board, since it has final authority","Give each group raw data so they can draw their own conclusions without any summary"],0,"Each audience makes different decisions: the board needs risk and strategy, IT managers need control status, and the SOC needs operational detail. One report for all either overwhelms executives or starves technical teams.","Job practice 3: Program communications",[null,"A single detailed report overwhelms executives and hides the key messages.","IT managers and the SOC need information to act; reporting only to the board leaves them uninformed.","Raw data shifts analysis to people who may lack the time or context to interpret it."],3],["x44",0,4,"An employee reports a lost laptop that contained customer data. The laptop had full-disk encryption enabled. What should the incident response team do FIRST?",["Notify all customers immediately that their data was breached","Close the incident because encrypted laptops cannot cause a breach under any circumstances","Confirm encryption was active and assess whether the data was actually exposed","Discipline the employee before gathering any facts"],2,"The team should verify encryption status and other facts to decide whether data was exposed, which drives notification obligations. Notifying all customers before assessing may be unnecessary and alarming.","Job practice 4: Incident triage",["Notifying before assessing may be unnecessary and causes needless alarm and cost.","Encryption may have been disabled or the device may have been unlocked, so facts must be confirmed.",null,"Disciplinary action before fact-finding is premature and discourages reporting."],2],["x45",0,4,"During a major incident, the technical team wants to rebuild a compromised server immediately, while legal wants to preserve evidence for possible litigation. What is the BEST approach?",["Rebuild immediately because service restoration always comes first","Leave the server running untouched until the litigation is resolved","Let the technical team decide alone because it is a technical issue","Capture forensic images and relevant logs, then rebuild the server"],3,"Taking forensic images and preserving logs before rebuilding meets both needs: evidence is kept and service can be restored. Leaving the server untouched indefinitely would prolong the outage and the attacker's access.","Job practice 4: Evidence preservation",["Rebuilding without preserving evidence can destroy information needed for legal action.","Leaving a compromised server running indefinitely extends both the outage and the risk.","Evidence and legal needs make this a business decision, not only a technical one.",null],3],["x46",0,4,"Which phase of incident response focuses on limiting the spread and impact of an incident?",["Preparation","Lessons learned","Containment","Detection"],2,"Containment stops the incident from spreading and limits damage, for example by isolating affected systems. Detection identifies that an incident is happening.","Job practice 4: Incident response phases",["Preparation happens before incidents and builds capability.","Lessons learned happens after recovery to improve future response.",null,"Detection identifies the incident but does not limit its spread."],1],["x47",0,4,"An organization's business continuity plan assumes staff can work from home during a crisis, but the VPN supports only 20% of employees at once. What does this reveal?",["That the VPN vendor breached its contract","A dependency gap that should have been identified in continuity planning and testing","That working from home is never a valid continuity strategy for any type of organization","That the plan should drop remote work entirely"],1,"Continuity strategies must be validated against the resources they depend on; testing at scale would reveal VPN capacity limits. Remote work can be valid if capacity is planned.","Job practice 4: Business continuity planning",["The VPN may be performing as specified; the plan did not account for its limits.",null,"Remote work is a common and valid strategy when supporting resources are sized properly.","Dropping remote work removes a useful option instead of fixing the capacity gap."],2],["x48",0,4,"An attacker's persistence mechanism was missed during eradication, and the attacker regained access a week after recovery. What should the post-incident review MOST likely recommend?",["Improving scoping and validation steps before declaring eradication complete","Skipping eradication in future incidents to recover faster and reduce the cost of each response","Replacing the incident response team with an external firm","Reducing monitoring after recovery to avoid false alarms"],0,"Reinfection shows that scoping and verification of eradication were incomplete; the process should add steps such as threat hunting and validation before recovery is declared. Replacing the team may not fix the process gap.","Job practice 4: Eradication and recovery",[null,"Skipping eradication would make reinfection even more likely.","Changing providers does not by itself fix a missing validation step in the process.","Monitoring should increase after recovery to detect any return of the attacker."],3],["x49",0,4,"Which is the BEST reason to define an incident communication plan in advance?",["So every employee can speak to the media about the incident","So the organization can avoid ever disclosing incidents","So messages to staff, customers, regulators and media are accurate, timely and authorized","So technical details are shared publicly as quickly as possible"],2,"A communication plan defines who speaks, to whom and when, so messages are consistent, accurate and approved. Allowing anyone to speak to the media leads to conflicting and possibly harmful statements.","Job practice 4: Incident communications",["Uncontrolled media contact creates inconsistent messages and legal exposure.","Many laws require disclosure; the plan supports appropriate disclosure rather than avoidance.",null,"Releasing technical details too early can aid attackers and spread inaccurate information."],2],["x50",0,4,"A tabletop exercise reveals that no one knows who can authorize shutting down the e-commerce site during an attack. What should the information security manager do?",["Define and document decision authority in the incident response plan and brief the relevant executives","Give the SOC analyst on shift authority to shut down any system","Wait for a real incident to see who steps forward","Remove the e-commerce site from the scope of the plan"],0,"Decision rights for high-impact actions should be defined in advance, usually with business leadership, and communicated so there is no delay during an incident. Giving any analyst unlimited shutdown authority ignores the business impact of such decisions.","Job practice 4: Incident response plan",[null,"Shutting down revenue systems has major business impact and needs appropriate authority, not any analyst on shift.","Waiting for a real incident wastes the lesson the exercise just revealed.","Excluding a critical system from the plan leaves it without any response guidance."],3],["x51",0,4,"What is maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)?",["The average time it takes to repair a failed component","The amount of data that can be lost without harm","The time between two consecutive backups","The longest a process can be unavailable before the organization suffers unacceptable harm"],3,"MTD is the outer limit of how long a business process can be down before the damage is unacceptable; RTO must be set shorter than MTD. The amount of data that can be lost relates to RPO.","Job practice 4: Business impact analysis",["The average repair time is mean time to repair, not MTD.","Acceptable data loss is measured by the RPO.","The interval between backups is a backup schedule parameter, not MTD.",null],2],["x52",0,4,"A security analyst notices unusual outbound traffic from a database server at 3 a.m. to an unknown external address. The incident response plan is unclear on severity. What should drive the severity rating?",["The volume of network traffic observed in bytes","The sensitivity of the data on the server and the potential business impact","The number of analysts currently on shift","Whether the external address is located in another country or in a region with weak privacy laws"],1,"Severity should reflect potential business impact, such as exposure of sensitive data, which guides escalation and response speed. Traffic volume or destination country can be indicators, but they are not the basis for rating impact.","Job practice 4: Incident classification",["Traffic volume is an indicator, but small transfers of sensitive data can be very severe.",null,"Staffing levels affect capacity to respond, not how severe the incident is.","Destination geography is a clue for investigation, not a measure of business impact."],3],["x53",0,4,"Why should the disaster recovery plan be stored in a location that remains accessible if the primary data center fails?",["So auditors can review it at any time without asking permission during the annual audit cycle","So the recovery team can follow it when primary systems are unavailable","So that it is automatically updated by the production systems","So that only the IT director can read it"],1,"If the plan is stored only on systems in the failed data center, the team may not be able to access it when it is needed most. Accessibility during a disaster is the main reason for offsite or out-of-band storage.","Job practice 4: Disaster recovery planning",["Auditor access is a minor benefit compared with the recovery team being able to use the plan.",null,"Plans are maintained through a change process, not automatically updated by production systems.","Restricting the plan to one person creates a single point of failure during recovery."],2],["x54",0,4,"Ransomware encrypted file servers. Backups exist, but the attacker had admin access for weeks. Management asks how soon systems can be restored. What should the information security manager emphasize?",["Restore from the newest backup immediately to minimize downtime","Pay the ransom first because it is always faster than restoring","Restore everything at once and investigate the cause later","Restoration must wait until backups are verified clean and attacker access is removed, even if it takes longer"],3,"With weeks of attacker access, recent backups may contain malware or backdoors, and restoring before removing access risks immediate reinfection. Speed matters, but restoring compromised systems can restart the incident.","Job practice 4: Recovery after ransomware",["The newest backup may already contain the attacker's persistence and lead to reinfection.","Paying does not guarantee working decryption or removal of the attacker, and it may carry legal issues.","Restoring before investigating risks bringing the same compromise back.",null],3],["x55",0,4,"Which exercise involves actually switching operations to the recovery site while the primary site is shut down?",["Full interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Checklist review","Structured walkthrough"],0,"A full interruption test stops primary operations and runs them at the recovery site, giving the most realistic results but also the highest risk. A tabletop exercise is discussion-based with no systems affected.","Job practice 4: Plan testing",[null,"A tabletop exercise is a discussion and does not affect systems.","A checklist review confirms plan contents and resources on paper.","A structured walkthrough steps through the plan with the team but does not switch operations."],1],["x56",0,4,"An incident involves a cloud provider's platform that hosts the organization's customer portal. What should the incident response plan include to handle this?",["A requirement that the organization investigate the provider's hardware directly","A statement that cloud incidents are entirely the provider's responsibility","Provider contacts, shared responsibilities and agreed notification timelines","A plan to migrate to another provider during every incident"],2,"Cloud incidents need coordination with the provider, so the plan should include contacts, the shared responsibility split and contractual notification terms. Assuming the provider handles everything ignores the customer's own responsibilities.","Job practice 4: Incident management with third parties",["Customers usually cannot access provider hardware; coordination happens through agreed channels.","Under shared responsibility the customer still owns parts of the response, such as its data and accounts.",null,"Migrating providers during an incident is impractical and adds risk."],2],["x57",0,4,"After a data breach, the organization must decide whether to notify regulators. The facts are still incomplete, but the legal deadline is approaching. What is the BEST action?",["Wait until every detail is confirmed, even if the deadline passes and the investigation is closed","Decide not to notify because the facts are incomplete","Let the IT team send the notification without legal review","Notify within the deadline with known facts and provide updates as the investigation continues"],3,"Many regulations allow or expect an initial notification with available facts followed by updates, so missing the deadline while waiting for certainty creates additional violations. Legal review should still be part of the process.","Job practice 4: Regulatory notification",["Missing a legal deadline adds a compliance violation on top of the breach.","Incomplete facts do not remove the obligation to notify when thresholds are met.","Notifications have legal consequences and should be reviewed by legal counsel.",null],3],["x58",0,4,"Which is the MAIN purpose of a playbook for a common incident type such as phishing?",["To replace the incident response plan entirely","To document the history of every past phishing campaign","To give responders consistent, tested steps that speed up and standardize the response","To assign blame to users who report phishing emails"],2,"Playbooks provide specific, repeatable steps for common scenarios, which improves speed and consistency and supports automation. They work within the broader incident response plan rather than replacing it.","Job practice 4: Incident response procedures",["Playbooks support the plan; the plan still defines governance, roles and escalation.","A historical record is useful for analysis but is not what a playbook is for.",null,"Users who report phishing should be encouraged, not blamed."],2],["x59",0,4,"The incident response team keeps missing its target time to contain incidents. Analysis shows most delay occurs waiting for approvals to isolate systems. What is the BEST improvement?",["Hire more analysts to detect incidents faster","Pre-approve containment actions for defined scenarios with clear criteria","Remove containment from the incident response process for defined high-severity incidents","Extend the target time so it is easier to meet"],1,"Since the bottleneck is approval, pre-authorizing specific containment actions under defined conditions directly removes the delay while keeping control. Hiring more analysts improves detection, which is not where the delay occurs.","Job practice 4: Incident response metrics and improvement",["More analysts would not speed up approvals, which is where the delay lies.",null,"Removing containment would allow incidents to spread further.","Moving the target hides the problem instead of improving response."],3],["x60",0,4,"What is the MAIN difference between an event and an incident?",["An incident is an event that harms or threatens the security of information assets","An event is always more serious than an incident","Incidents are logged automatically, while events are reported by people or by the help desk","There is no difference; the terms are used interchangeably"],0,"An event is any observable occurrence, while an incident is an event that actually or potentially compromises confidentiality, integrity or availability. Most events are benign and never become incidents.","Job practice 4: Incident definitions",[null,"Events are usually less serious; many are routine and harmless.","Both events and incidents can be detected by tools or reported by people.","The distinction matters because it drives when the response process is triggered."],2]]);
